+### Named tests
+
+`tests:` lists the test cases that prove a task. Each entry is `<file> :: <case>`.
+
+- **Grammar.** The raw entry is taken literally and split at the first ` :: `. `<file>` is trimmed and has any leading `./` removed; `<case>` is trimmed. Two entries are the same when their normalised pairs are the same.
+- **Unique names.** A case name must be unique in its file, because a name that appears twice cannot tell which case passed.
+- **Matching.** The test command runs once per test file. A line that starts with `test_pass_prefix` followed by the case name, and nothing more, is a pass line. A line that starts with `test_fail_prefix` followed by the case name, then either the end of the line or ` (` and detail, is a fail line. A case is `passed` when only pass lines name it, `failed` when only fail lines name it, `absent` when no line names it, and `ambiguous` when both kinds do or either kind repeats.
+- **`base_sha`.** `plan-state set <plan> <id> running` writes `base_sha:` the first time a task goes to `running`: the commit the task started from. A resume keeps the value it has, so the check still compares against the original base.
+- **Labels.** `verify-tests` reports one label per entry. `ok`: the case failed or was absent at `base_sha` and passes now. `ok-absent`: the case did not exist at `base_sha` and passes now. `not-red`: the case already passed at `base_sha`, so it proves nothing new. `not-green`: the case does not pass now. `ambiguous`: the output names the case more than once or as both passed and failed.
